================================================================================
BUG #2: Wrong bv() Helper Type - NIBBLER ONLY
================================================================================

Root cause: Nibbler's bv(int val) causes 64-bit hex literals like 0xFFFFFFFE
            to overflow/truncate incorrectly. Other models use uint64_t.

Fix: (1) Change helper signature int → uint64_t
     (2) Add explicit cast at JALR instruction for safety

================================================================================
PATCH 4B/5: Nibbler - Fix bv() Helper Type
================================================================================
File: IMDb/IMDb_Cores/ILA_Nibbler/nibbler_verification/include/riscvIla.hpp
Line: 48

--- a/IMDb/IMDb_Cores/ILA_Nibbler/nibbler_verification/include/riscvIla.hpp
+++ b/IMDb/IMDb_Cores/ILA_Nibbler/nibbler_verification/include/riscvIla.hpp
@@ -45,7 +45,8 @@
   ExprRef indexIntoGPR(const ExprRef& idxBits);
   void UpdateGPR(InstrRef& inst, const ExprRef& idxBits, const ExprRef& val);

-  ExprRef bv(int val) { return BvConst(val, XLEN); }
+  // FIX: Use uint64_t instead of int to handle large hex literals like 0xFFFFFFFE
+  ExprRef bv(uint64_t val) { return BvConst(val, XLEN); }
   ExprRef zext(const ExprRef& v) { return ZExt(v, XLEN); }
   ExprRef sext(const ExprRef& v) { return SExt(v, XLEN); }

================================================================================
PATCH 5/5: Nibbler - Add Explicit Cast at JALR
================================================================================
File: IMDb/IMDb_Cores/ILA_Nibbler/nibbler_verification/src/riscvIla.cc
Line: 214

--- a/IMDb/IMDb_Cores/ILA_Nibbler/nibbler_verification/src/riscvIla.cc
+++ b/IMDb/IMDb_Cores/ILA_Nibbler/nibbler_verification/src/riscvIla.cc
@@ -211,7 +211,8 @@
       auto decode = (opcode == JALR);
       instr.SetDecode(decode);

-      instr.SetUpdate(pc, (rs1_val + immI) & bv(0xFFFFFFFE));
+      // FIX: Cast to uint32_t to ensure 32-bit literal, not 64-bit
+      instr.SetUpdate(pc, (rs1_val + immI) & bv((uint32_t)0xFFFFFFFE));
       UPDATE_R(rd, NC);
       RECORD_INST("JALR");
     }

================================================================================
WHY THE TYPE MATTERS
================================================================================

When passing 0xFFFFFFFE to a function:

With int parameter (Nibbler before):
  0xFFFFFFFE → interpreted as 64-bit unsigned → 18446744073709551614
  → conversion to int (32-bit signed) → overflow/undefined
  → BvConst assertion failure

With uint64_t parameter (all models after):
  0xFFFFFFFE → unsigned literal → 4294967294 (fits in 32 bits)
  → safe conversion to uint64_t
  → BvConst validation passes ✓

With explicit cast (uint32_t)0xFFFFFFFE:
  → Forces 32-bit interpretation
  → Most explicit and portable ✓✓

